// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  EYE BANDS - DERIVED FROM THE ART, NOT MEASURED BY HAND
//
//  ui/petfx.cpp used to carry a 24-row table of blink bands read off the
//  decoded art BY EYE, 1,100 lines away from the pixels it indexed, frozen
//  by static_assert(SPRITE_REV == 1) because nothing else could notice when
//  the two drifted. Sixty bodies would have made it sixty rows of the same.
//  These come out of the same .txt files as the pixels, by one stated rule -
//  an eye is an enclosed HOLE, and THE EYES are the biggest group of holes
//  that can be closed together without closing anything that is not a hole -
//  so they cannot drift from the art, and what the rule gets wrong is written
//  down in gen_sprites.py's eye_band() rather than discovered.
//
//  THE GUARD IS NOT HERE. The band is only half the blink; the other half is
//  ui/petfx_core.cpp's pf_build_lids(), and until P9-C6 that function was in
//  a translation unit no host binary could link, so the composited blink
//  frame was a picture nothing in the tree had ever drawn. It is now:
//  tests/test_sprite_pipeline.cpp runs the shipped fill over the shipped
//  atlas, and tests/bin/sprite_dump blink NAME draws it for a human.
//
//  { 255, 0, 0, 0 } means THIS BODY DOES NOT BLINK: y1 < y0, which
//  pf_build_lids() already answers 0 for. It is what a body with no
//  small enclosed hole gets, and it is a legal answer.
